"""Book 16 (The Desk and the Firm) chapter briefs: data for tools/make_briefs.py --book 16.

`uses` lists terms defined elsewhere that the chapter relies on (not redefined); owners in parentheses:
(B7.18) = One Quant Book 7, chapter 18, checked against the multi-line \\index{} harvest of Books 1-13 on
2026-09-28 (2,753 definitions); (B14.9) / (B15.22) = a batch book, by its outline (to be confirmed at the sync);
(ch5) = an earlier chapter of this book.

Ownership. Books 1-13 keep their terms. Book 1 owns the firm types (proprietary trading firm, hedge fund,
multi-manager platform, asset manager, assets under management), the fund fees (management fee, performance
fee, high-water mark), the sell-side vocabulary (franchise, prime services, risk-weighted assets), financing
(leverage, prime broker, haircut, rehypothecation) and the access vocabulary (exchange membership, clearing
member, give-up, volume tier, incentive programme, market-maker appointment); Book 2 the master agreements
(ISDA, GMRA), the credit support annex and the drawdown/Kelly vocabulary; Book 6 the risk and control
vocabulary (risk limit, risk hierarchy, VaR/ES, stress test, Euler allocation, hurdle rate, funds-transfer
pricing, three lines of defence, model risk, product control, IPV, operational risk, rogue trading,
segregation of duties, close-out netting); Book 7 the research-process terms (research log, stage gate, kill
criterion, research review, crowding); Book 8 pod, multi-strategy book and drawdown limit; Book 9 the bank
desks (franchise P&L, central risk book) and the manipulation terms; Book 11 the unit economics of market
making (revenue capture, capture decay) and the risk-control policy (loss limit, kill switch, market access
rule). In the batch, Book 14 owns connectivity products and contract terms (latency tier, cross-connect,
service-level agreement, DR sites); Book 15 owns data systems, post-trade systems (reconciliation break,
confirmation, entitlements) and engineering practice (on-call rotation, blameless review, incident response).
This book owns business-model, organisation, control-function, funding/treasury, legal-documentation,
compensation-policy and firm-strategy terms.

No strategy files (the outline gives Book 16 none). Code is Python only: firm-level models, deterministic and
offline. Public figures (filings, regulator statistics, the case studies' records) are committed as small
derived tables under data/desk/ with LICENSES.md; every named-firm line has a ledger row; criticism only from
court, regulator or official-inquiry records, stated neutrally with the outcome. Tax and regulatory specifics
live in dated boxes; running text states the durable mechanism. The book gives no legal, tax or investment
advice: it explains how things work and where the rules are.

Case studies already told elsewhere in the series are re-read here for the firm-level decisions only, with
prose pointers: August 2007 (Book 7 ch. 28, Book 8 ch. 1 and 28), the 2012 software incident (Book 11 ch. 27,
Book 13 ch. 22), Archegos (Book 1 ch. 6, Book 6 ch. 22), FTX (Book 3 ch. 15), LME nickel (Book 3 ch. 8 and 28),
the London Whale (Book 6 ch. 26-27), rogue trading (Book 6 ch. 28), cum-ex (Book 1 ch. 16).

Running project (code/firm/, all new and unique; checked against the 378 existing directories on 2026-09-28):
  firmecon (ch1)  partnership (ch2)  podshop (ch3)  fundterms (ch4)  bankdesk (ch5)  deskplan (ch6)
  limitalloc (ch7)  ddrules (ch8)  projsel (ch9)  bonuspool (ch10)  gardenleave (ch11, analytical tool)
  escalation (ch12)  opsmetrics (ch13)  treasury (ch14)  signoff (ch15)  compliance (ch16)
  regmap (ch17, analytical tool)  docterms (ch18, analytical tool)  techtier (ch19)  buildbuy (ch20)
  teamtopo (ch21)  databudget (ch22)  dealterms (ch23)  entryplan (ch24)  fundlaunch (ch25)
  decisionlog (ch26)  crisisdrill (ch27)  casebook (ch28-29)  moats (ch30)
All Python; they build on mmecon, multistrat, perf, fees, feesched, clearcost, capacity, marginspiral,
initmargin, margin, riskctl, surveil, queues, combine, multitest, pnl, pnlexplain, latbudget, vendoreval.
"""
